{%- comment -%}
  App Embed Block (target: head) — emits Open Graph + Twitter Card meta tags
  from NATIVE Liquid objects (page_title/page_description/canonical_url/
  product/shop), so social-preview cards (Facebook, X/Twitter, LinkedIn,
  Slack, WhatsApp, and ChatGPT/Perplexity link unfurls) get a title,
  description and image with no admin API call and no per-item sync.

  Fallback chains (plan §C4):
    title:       page_title       → product.title           → shop.name
    description: page_description → shop.description
    image:       product.featured_image → block default_og_image setting
                 → shop.brand.logo
  Product pages may override title/description/image via the metafields
  `custom.og_description` / `custom.og_image` when present.

  All values are HTML *attribute* content (not JSON), so they use `| escape`
  rather than `| json`.

  NOTE: app-embed blocks conventionally target "body" in this extension (see
  structured-data.liquid); OG/Twitter tags need to live in <head>, which this
  block requests via "target": "head". Verify placement in the theme editor
  after install — if a theme rejects head-targeted app blocks, this falls
  back to being included wherever the theme renders app-embed-head content.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign is_product = false
  if request.page_type == 'product' and product
    assign is_product = true
  endif

  assign og_title = page_title | default: product.title | default: shop.name
  assign og_description = page_description | default: shop.description

  assign og_image = ''
  if is_product and product.featured_image
    assign og_image = product.featured_image | image_url: width: 1200
  elsif block.settings.default_og_image
    assign og_image = block.settings.default_og_image | image_url: width: 1200
  elsif shop.brand.logo
    assign og_image = shop.brand.logo | image_url: width: 1200
  endif

  # Metafield overrides (product pages only).
  if is_product
    if product.metafields.custom.og_description.value != blank
      assign og_description = product.metafields.custom.og_description.value
    endif
    if product.metafields.custom.og_image.value != blank
      assign og_image = product.metafields.custom.og_image.value | image_url: width: 1200
    endif
  endif

  assign og_type = 'website'
  if is_product
    assign og_type = 'product'
  elsif request.page_type == 'article'
    assign og_type = 'article'
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  Every tag carries data-contentpilot, the same marker structured-data.liquid
  puts on its JSON-LD blocks and for the same reason: most themes set og:title
  and og:image themselves, and the delivered HTML otherwise cannot tell a
  merchant which of two og:image tags is ours. `data-*` is valid on <meta> and
  inert for every consumer -- Facebook, X, LinkedIn and Slack read `property`/
  `name` and `content`, and ignore the rest.
{%- endcomment -%}
<meta property="og:title" content="{{ og_title | escape }}" data-contentpilot="og">
{%- if og_description != blank -%}
<meta property="og:description" content="{{ og_description | escape }}" data-contentpilot="og">
{%- endif -%}
<meta property="og:url" content="{{ canonical_url | escape }}" data-contentpilot="og">
<meta property="og:type" content="{{ og_type | escape }}" data-contentpilot="og">
{%- if og_image != blank -%}
<meta property="og:image" content="{{ og_image | escape }}" data-contentpilot="og">
{%- endif -%}

<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" data-contentpilot="og">
<meta name="twitter:title" content="{{ og_title | escape }}" data-contentpilot="og">
{%- if og_description != blank -%}
<meta name="twitter:description" content="{{ og_description | escape }}" data-contentpilot="og">
{%- endif -%}
{%- if og_image != blank -%}
<meta name="twitter:image" content="{{ og_image | escape }}" data-contentpilot="og">
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Open Graph / Twitter",
  "target": "head",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Adds Open Graph and Twitter Card meta tags so shared links show a title, description and image on Facebook, X/Twitter, LinkedIn, Slack, WhatsApp and AI chat link previews. Enable once - no placement needed. Most themes already set og:title and og:image themselves; the app's Structured data section, step 3, measures which tags your pages already serve and whether switching this on would duplicate them."
    },
    {
      "type": "image_picker",
      "id": "default_og_image",
      "label": "Default share image (used when a page has no image of its own)"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
